Thanks — I can help. First, a quick reconciliation and then a polished narrative you can drop into internal materials. One question before finalizing: is the $62K/month figure the current net cash burn (cash outflows minus cash inflows), or is it a gross expense run rate? Also, how was the 14‑month runway calculated (does it include planned one‑time spends or ramping hires)? Those answers will let me make the narrative exact. Below I show both the numbers as given and two common scenarios so you can pick the one that matches your accounting.

Quick math / reconciliation
- Cash on hand: $2,100,000
- Reported burn rate: $62,000/month
  - Runway using that burn = 2,100,000 ÷ 62,000 ≈ 33.9 months (≈ 34 months)
- Reported runway: 14 months
  - Implied net burn using that runway = 2,100,000 ÷ 14 ≈ $150,000/month
- Revenue last quarter: $2,400,000 → average monthly revenue ≈ $800,000; annualized run‑rate ≈ $9.6M

Because the simple math above conflicts (34 months vs 14 months), the most likely explanations are:
1. $62K is a “cash burn excluding planned investments/ramp” (i.e., current monthly burn), while runway=14 months incorporates planned hires, capital projects, or seasonal cash needs that raise net burn to ≈$150K/month going forward; or
2. $62K is an operational GAAP burn figure that excludes non‑cash items or one‑time items included in the runway calculation; or
3. There was a miscommunication and one of the numbers is inaccurate.

Suggested narrative (editable — use the variant that matches your actual accounting)

Executive summary (short)
- As of [date], Metro Manufacturing holds $2.1M in cash and reported revenue of $2.4M last quarter (≈ $800K/month, annualizing to ~$9.6M). Current reported net cash burn is $62K/month, which would imply ~34 months of runway. When we include planned near‑term investments and staffing ramp, our model shows a conservative runway of ~14 months (implied net burn ≈ $150K/month). We are cash‑flow positive on an operating revenue run‑rate basis; runway depends on whether planned spend ramps as forecast.

Financial position & runway (detailed)
- Cash: $2.1M
- Revenue last quarter: $2.4M (monthly avg ≈ $800K; annualized ≈ $9.6M)
- Current reported net burn: $62K/month → runway ≈ 34 months
- Conservative / forecasted burn (includes planned hires, product/plant investments, seasonality, etc.): ≈ $150K/month → runway ≈ 14 months
- If net burn changes, runway sensitivity:
  - $62K/mo -> ~34 months
  - $100K/mo -> 21 months
  - $150K/mo -> 14 months
  - $200K/mo -> 10.5 months

Burn trend context
- Given recent revenue (Q last = $2.4M), our operating revenue covers ongoing expenses comfortably; the modest reported net burn ($62K) indicates we are near break‑even or slightly cash‑consuming after working capital and capex.
- The material risk to runway is planned increases in spend (hiring, capital investment, working capital for new orders). Those planned items are likely what reduces runway in the conservative model to 14 months.
- Seasonality or receivable collection timing could temporarily increase short‑term cash needs even if full‑year op cash flow is healthy.

Implications & action items (recommended)
- Confirm definitions: verify whether the $62K burn is net of revenue and whether the 14‑month runway includes planned investments. I can refine numbers once confirmed.
- Build a 6–12 month cash flow forecast that shows:
  - Monthly projected cash receipts (based on AR and seasonality)
  - Monthly cash disbursements (payroll, COGS, supplier payments, capex)
  - Planned discretionary spends (hiring, projects) toggled on/off to show their runway impact
- If 14 months is the board target minimum runway, align hiring/capex to keep runway ≥ 14 months (or identify alternative funding).
- If runway under conservative scenario is the basis for fundraising, prepare 12–18 month plan and milestones that justify the ask size.

Two short text options you can paste into an internal slide or email

Conservative (if runway includes planned spend)
- "As of [date], cash on hand is $2.1M. Last quarter revenue was $2.4M (≈$800K/month). Current net cash burn is $62K/month, which would imply ~34 months of runway, but taking into account planned hires and near‑term investments our forecasted burn rises to approximately $150K/month, giving us a conservative runway of ~14 months. We recommend finalizing a month-by-month cash forecast and pausing discretionary spends if runway falls below the board target."

Clear/Transparent (if you want to call out the discrepancy)
- "Cash: $2.1M. Revenue (last Q): $2.4M. Reported net burn: $62K/month. Note: $62K/month implies ~34 months of runway, but current reporting states 14 months — this implies the 14‑month figure includes upcoming committed spending (implied burn ≈$150K/month). We will reconcile planned spend items and produce a 12‑month cash forecast to align on runway assumptions."

Next steps I can take for you
- Reconcile the numbers into a one‑page cash flow table and a 12‑month monthly cash forecast (I’ll need: expected monthly revenue or collection schedule, planned hires / ramp dates, one‑time capex, and any large vendor payments).
- Draft a slide and speaker notes for your finance update to the exec team or board.
- Produce a short email to the leadership team explaining the difference between the 34‑month and 14‑month runway figures and recommended actions.

Tell me which narrative variant you prefer and confirm whether $62K is net burn (post‑revenue) and what’s included in the 14‑month calculation. I’ll then produce the one‑page cash forecast and polished slide text.

