Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Short version — key messaging you can use immediately
- Cash on hand: $2.1M.
- Last quarter revenue: $2.4M (~$800K/month).
- Reported net burn: $62K/month → implied runway = 2.1M ÷ 62K ≈ 33.9 months.
- Reported runway: 14 months → implies a net burn of 2.1M ÷ 14 ≈ $150K/month.
- Note: these two statements (62K burn and 14‑month runway) are inconsistent — reconcile which figure is the company’s current operating/net burn (see below).

Why this matters (short narrative for internal materials)
- At the stated net burn of $62K/month we have a very healthy runway (~34 months), giving optionality for investment in growth or hiring.
- If the finance team is using a 14‑month runway, the business is assuming a materially higher monthly cash outflow (~$150K/month) — likely because of one‑time items, committed hires, marketing/GTM ramp, debt service, capex, or conservative reserves. That changes prioritization and fundraising timing.
- Cash vs revenue context: cash (2.1M) covers ~2.6 months of current monthly revenue (2.1M ÷ $800K ≈ 2.63 months). That’s useful to show operating leverage: revenue is large relative to net burn.

Concrete calculations (so you can paste numbers into slides)
- Monthly revenue (last quarter): 2.4M ÷ 3 = $800K/month.
- Runway if net burn = $62K/month: 2.1M ÷ 62K ≈ 33.9 months.
- Implied net burn if runway = 14 months: 2.1M ÷ 14 = $150K/month.
- Cash relative to monthly revenue: 2.1M ÷ 800K ≈ 2.63 months of revenue.
- Quick “stress” examples:
  - If burn rises to $150K/month → runway ≈ 14 months.
  - If burn rises to $200K/month → runway ≈ 10.5 months.
  - If burn rises to $300K/month → runway ≈ 7 months.

Suggested finance narrative for internal/board materials (two lengths)

1) One‑paragraph (for executive summary)
“Metro Manufacturing holds $2.1M in cash. Last quarter revenue totaled $2.4M (≈ $800K/month). Our currently reported net burn is $62K/month, which would yield ~34 months of runway; however our planning runway figure of 14 months implies a materially higher monthly cash outflow (~$150K/month). The difference appears driven by planned growth investments and/or one‑time commitments — we are reconciling those items. Under the conservative plan (14‑month runway) we are prioritizing non‑critical discretionary spend, phasing hiring, and preparing a targeted financing timeline to preserve optionality.”

2) Two‑paragraph (for finance appendix)
“Current position: Cash balance $2.1M; run‑rate revenue ~ $800K/month (Q last quarter $2.4M). Reported net cash burn is $62K/month which, on a straight line basis, implies ~34 months of runway. The planning runway figure used elsewhere (14 months) implies an effective burn of ~ $150K/month — this gap reflects planned operating investments (hiring, GTM, capex), committed cash outflows (debt, leases), and/or conservative buffers built into the forecast.

Action plan and implications: We will (1) reconcile reported burn vs planning burn and produce a monthly 12–18 month cash flow forecast showing committed vs discretionary spend; (2) model three scenarios — Base (current net burn ~ $62K), Growth (ramp to implied planning burn ~ $150K), and Downside (revenue compression/increased costs) — and present the impact on runway and milestone timing; (3) recommend cost phasing or temporary deferrals on non‑critical spends if runway shortens below 12 months. Based on the reconciled plan we will update the fundraising timeline and recommended target amounts.”

What to reconcile right away (so you can close the inconsistency)
- Confirm the definition of “burn rate” used: gross burn (all outflows) vs net burn (outflows minus revenue). Given revenue is large ($800K/mo), net burn may be small.
- Check for one‑time or committed items included in the 14‑month runway: planned hires, marketing spend, product capex, bonuses, lease obligations, debt service, taxes, vendor prepayments.
- Confirm whether runway calculation assumes revenue growth/decline or future financing intentionally excluded.
- Ask treasury/FP&A for the monthly cash flow schedule being used to calculate 14 months — get the line‑item drivers.

Recommended charts & metrics to include on the slide or appendix
- Monthly cash balance projection (12–18 months) for Base / Growth / Downside scenarios.
- Monthly burn (gross and net) vs monthly revenue.
- Runway months under each scenario.
- Implied burn reconciliation (show math: cash ÷ runway = implied monthly burn).
- Key assumptions list (hiring, marketing, one‑time spend, revenue growth).

Tactical next steps I can do for you
