Immediate math check
- Runway using net burn = cash / net burn = $2.1M / $62K ≈ 33.9 months (≈ 34 months).
- If runway = 14 months, implied monthly net burn = $2.1M / 14 ≈ $150K / month.

So: $62K net burn → ~34 months runway. A reported 14 months runway implies a materially higher monthly net burn (~$150K). We need to confirm which definition/assumptions produced the “14 months” number.

Possible reasons for the mismatch
- Different definitions used:
  - “Burn rate = $62K” might be net burn (cash outflow after cash receipts). That gives ~34 months runway.
  - “Runway = 14 months” might have been calculated using a forward-looking model that includes planned hiring, one-time investments, commitments, or assumes lower near-term cash collections (conservative scenario).
- Revenue recognition vs cash collection: $2.4M last quarter may not be equal to cash collected—AR timing can change short-term runway.
- Gross vs net: you may be tracking gross cash outflows (expenses) separately; e.g., if monthly operating outflows are ~862K and revenue is 800K, net burn is 62K (consistent), but someone might have computed runway differently (e.g., cash / planned gross spend).
- Upcoming one-offs (debt repayments, capex, earn-outs) or committed spend that reduce available runway.

