Good — this is straightforward to turn into a concise, accurate finance narrative. Below I (1) call out an important data-consistency point, (2) give the quick math, (3) offer three scenario-driven cash/runway calculations, and (4) provide a short, board-ready narrative + recommended next steps and KPIs you can drop into internal materials.

1) Important check (please confirm)
- The numbers you gave are internally inconsistent as stated. If cash = $2.1M and burn = $62K/month, simple math gives ~34 months of runway (2,100,000 ÷ 62,000 ≈ 33.9 months). You also stated “14 months runway.” That implies a different effective monthly cash outflow (2,100,000 ÷ 14 ≈ $150K/month) or that the 14 months includes one‑time, committed outflows (capex, debt repayments, inventory build, acquisitions, taxes, etc.) or materially different cash conversion assumptions.
- Ask: Which is correct?
  - Is $62K/month the net cash burn (cash outflow after operating receipts), or is it “operating burn” that excludes debt service, capex, planned investments, or working-capital build?
  - Does the 14‑month runway include committed one-time spends (e.g., planned capex, inventory purchases, earnouts, debt principal) or do you use a conservative runway method (e.g., stress-case revenue drops, AR delays)?

I’ll prepare the narrative assuming both interpretations — so you can pick the version that matches your internal accounting.

2) Quick math (transparent)
- Starting cash: $2,100,000
- Stated burn: $62,000/month → runway = 2,100,000 / 62,000 = 33.9 months (~34 months)
- Implied burn from 14‑month runway: 2,100,000 / 14 = $150,000/month (this is what your runway implies)
- Last quarter revenue: $2,400,000 → monthly average ≈ $800,000
  - If monthly revenue ≈ $800K, then:
    - $62K burn = ~7.8% of monthly revenue
    - $150K burn = ~18.8% of monthly revenue

3) Scenario snapshots (for slide or appendix)
- Base (use stated burn = $62K/mo)
  - Months until cash zero: 33.9 months
  - Cash after 6 months: 2.10M − 6×62K = 1.73M
  - Cash after 12 months: 2.10M − 12×62K = 1.36M
- Conservative (use implied runway = $150K/mo)
  - Months until cash zero: 14 months (by definition)
  - Cash after 6 months: 2.10M − 6×150K = 1.20M
  - Cash after 12 months: 2.10M − 12×150K = 0.30M
- Stress (illustrative downside; burn = $250K/mo)
  - Months until cash zero: 8.4 months
  - Useful for showing sensitivity to revenue disruption or major one‑time cash uses

Also show one-off spend impact (example):
- Planned capex or inventory build = $500K → adjusted starting cash = 1.6M
  - Runway at $62K burn: 1.6M / 62K ≈ 25.8 months
  - Runway at $150K burn: 1.6M / 150K ≈ 10.7 months

4) Concise, board‑ready narrative (pick the version matching your accounting)

Plain factual paragraph (if $62K is the true net burn)
- “As of [date], Metro Manufacturing holds $2.1M in cash. Our net cash burn is approximately $62K per month, giving us roughly 34 months of runway on current operating trends. Revenue in the most recent quarter was $2.4M (≈ $800K per month), so current cash outflows are under 8% of monthly revenues. This position gives us comfortable near‑term liquidity, but we continue to monitor working capital and any one‑time commitments that would reduce runway.”

Conservative paragraph (if 14 months is the figure you report to the board)
- “As of [date], Metro Manufacturing holds $2.1M in cash. Under current planning assumptions (which include upcoming committed items and a conservative view of collections and seasonality), our effective cash burn is roughly $150K/month, resulting in a 14‑month runway. Revenue last quarter was $2.4M (≈ $800K/month). We are therefore running at an effective cash outflow equal to ~19% of current monthly revenue; actions to preserve optionality include tightening discretionary spend, accelerating receivables, and prioritizing working capital efficiency.”

Suggested short bullet list for slides (Risks & Actions)
- Risks
  - AR timing: slower collections compress liquidity
  - One-time commitments (capex/debt) not included in operating burn
  - Revenue seasonality or customer concentration
- Immediate actions
  - Update weekly rolling 13‑week cash forecast; include committed one‑time uses
  - Prioritize AR collection: tighter payment terms, focus on top accounts
  - Review discretionary spend and defer noncritical capex
  - Revisit procurement and inventory build plans to reduce working capital
  - Evaluate financing options (revolver/bridge/term) if runway under 12 months

KPIs to include in the finance pack
- Cash balance (actual) and forecast (13-week + monthly 12 months)
- Gross burn (operating cash outflow) vs. net burn (including financing, capex)
- Runway under base, conservative, and stress scenarios
- Revenue (monthly and trailing 12 months), gross margin %, contribution margin
- DSO (days sales outstanding), DPO, inventory days
- Major upcoming cash events (debt service, payroll, tax, capex)

5) Recommended next steps (what I can do for you)
- I can draft a one‑page cash‑flow slide and a 2‑paragraph narrative tailored for the board that reconciles the $62K burn vs the 14‑month figure (if you confirm which is the “official” runway). OR
- I can build a 12‑month month-by-month cash-flow projection (simple Excel-style table) if you give me planned one‑time outflows, expected monthly revenue profile, and expected changes in AR/AP/inventory.

Which do you want next?
- Option A: Short slide-ready narrative + 3-line summary (I’ll prepare now)
- Option B: 12-month month-by-month cash projection (I’ll need: planned capex/debt payments, expected monthly revenue or growth rate, and any planned hires or cost increases)
- Or tell me which runway definition is the canonical one and I’ll produce the final text and numbers ready to paste into your finance pack.

