Which of those is the true picture? (Possible explanations below.) I’ll prepare a narrative for both interpretations and recommended next steps so you can pick the right tone once we confirm.

1) Quick calculations (for your materials)
- Runway formula: Runway (months) = Cash on hand / Monthly net cash burn.
- If burn = $62K → Runway ≈ 33.9 months.
- If runway = 14 months → Implied burn ≈ $150K/month.
- Revenue last quarter = $2.4M → Monthly revenue ≈ $800K.
- Annualized burn:
  - At $62K/mo → $744K per year.
  - At $150K/mo → $1.8M per year.
- Burn as % of monthly revenue:
  - $62K / $800K ≈ 7.8%
  - $150K / $800K ≈ 18.8%

2) Possible reasons for the mismatch (what to check)
- $62K might be gross cash operating burn excluding debt service, capex, one-time investments, payroll reserves, or tax payments. The 14-month runway may include those extra cash outflows (i.e., net cash burn ≈ $150K).
