- Drivers of burn: payroll and benefits, inventory build / working capital, SG&A, and any one-time restructuring or product investments. Call out the single largest driver(s).
- Variance vs. conservative scenario: If planned investments proceed, net burn could rise toward ~$150K/month, cutting runway to ~14 months.
- Collections and working capital impact: AR collection timing can materially change monthly cash flow — highlight any concentration or late collections.

Scenario table (text form — include in slide)
- Baseline: net burn = $62K/mo → runway ≈ 34 months.
- Conservative: net burn = $150K/mo (assumed planned spend) → runway ≈ 14 months.
- Downside (50% revenue drop, expenses stay ~950K): net burn ≈ $550K/mo → runway ≈ 3.8 months.
- Upside (revenue +20% or 20% expense reduction): moves company to net positive cash flow — runway not applicable (cash accumulates).

Recommended slides / visuals
- Cash balance timeline (last 12 months) with runway overlay under current burn and conservative burn.
- Waterfall of monthly cash flow: revenue → COGS → OpEx → net cash burn.
- Scenario sensitivity chart showing months of runway vs monthly net burn (x-axis = net burn rate; y-axis = runway).
- Table of key assumptions (revenue recognition vs cash collection, AR days, planned hires/capex, one-time cash items, debt covenants).

